{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_go6zxatasguzmytdkkjj6u4twihk2ax2mywwk6jldiuihulum3xa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src/src/lib.c` (174 lines) exports 8 functions. The critical property is that it's **stateful**: three file-scope `static`s (`accumulator=0`, `multiplier=1`, `operation_count=0`) are mutated by every operation and then branched on by `findrep`. So \"configuration\" here isn't flags — it's the reachable states of those statics, selected by call sequence.\n\n## Harness design\n\nBoth `.so` files are loaded via `libloading` and called only through `dlsym`, so the `#[no_mangle]` wrappers are under test. One subtlety mattered a lot: `dlopen` de-duplicates by `(dev, ino)`, so loading the same path twice **shares** the statics across tests. Every test therefore loads a fresh private *copy* of each `.so`, giving pristine statics on both sides. `smoke.rs::fresh_pair_resets_hidden_static_state` proves this actually works — without it, every stateful test would have been quietly meaningless.\n\n`findrep.rs` also runs an independent third model of the C control flow, so each call is checked three ways, and `branch_coverage_audit` asserts all six `findrep` branches were seen **both taken and not taken**.\n\n## Two real divergences found and fixed\n\n**1. `INT_MIN / -1` didn't trap.** `divide_multiplier` used `wrapping_div`, returning `INT_MIN`. The C guards only `b != 0`, so gcc's `idiv` raises `#DE` and the process dies with **SIGFPE**:\n```\nC    -> signal 8 (SIGFPE)\nRust -> exit 0, quotient=-2147483648\n```\nReachable purely through the public API (`multiply_with_multiplier(INT_MIN, 1)` then `divide_multiplier(_, -1)`) — not theoretical. Fixed with `c_idiv`, reproducing the same hardware fault via inline `idiv` on x86_64.\n\n**2. Null deref aborted instead of segfaulting (dev profile only).** Rust's default `debug-assertions` inserted a \"null pointer dereference\" precondition check in `c_strlen`, turning the C's SIGSEGV into a non-unwinding panic → **SIGABRT** (11 vs 6). Fixed by pinning `[profile.dev] debug-assertions = false, overflow-checks = false`, since the C is compiled with no such preconditions.\n\nBoth are error paths that happy-path testing cannot reach — they only surfaced because the fatal cases were run in child processes and compared on **exact terminating signal**, not \"both failed somehow\".\n\n## Results\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Symbol parity | 8/8, **empty diff**, 0 missing / 0 extra |\n| `ERRORS.md` | 31/31 rows, all with passing tests |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | 50/50 rows, randomized inputs with fixed seeds |\n| Configurations | 6/6 (debug + release × default / no-default / all-features) — **75 tests each, 0 failures** |\n\n`Cargo.toml` declares no `[features]`, so the feature cross-product is a single configuration; `verify.sh` still enumerates the power set generically (and covers both profiles) so it keeps working if features are added later.\n\nTwo notes on judgement calls worth flagging: `unreachable!`/`abort()` do appear in `src/lib.rs`, but only inside the deliberate SIGFPE trap helper — not stubs. And fix #2 changes a build profile rather than code; I judged that legitimate because the C has no such runtime checks, but it is a config-level fix rather than a translation fix, so it's called out explicitly in `ERRORS.md`. `c_src/` is unmodified."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":562,"cache_read_input_tokens":155396,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":562,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a40d4a42-7189-4a2f-b2fa-1ef6d94d1c98","uuid":"6f25a9bd-0443-480a-bdc9-3706d1091278","timestamp":"2026-08-28T01:13:59.779Z"}
